<Base lang="en-US" pageType="CollectionPage" title="Martín Fierro in English — Argentina’s Gaucho Epic" description="Discover Martín Fierro, Argentina’s famous gaucho poem by José Hernández. Explore its story, historical context, author, original Spanish text, and English translation sources.">
<div class="en-portal">
  <nav class="en-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Martín Fierro</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>English edition</span></nav>
  <div class="en-edition"><span>THE ENGLISH EDITION</span><span>ARGENTINE LITERATURE · HISTORY · CULTURE</span></div>
  <section class="en-hero" aria-labelledby="en-title">
    <div class="en-visual">
      <img src="/media/fotografias-v3/hero-gaucho-pampa.webp" width="1440" height="850" fetchpriority="high" alt="Digital reconstruction of a gaucho riding across the Argentine pampas at sunset"/>
      <p>EDITORIAL RECONSTRUCTION · NOT A HISTORICAL PHOTOGRAPH</p>
    </div>
    <div class="en-hero-copy">
      <p class="en-kicker">JOSÉ HERNÁNDEZ · 1872 / 1879</p>
      <h1 id="en-title">Martín Fierro: <em>the world of Argentina’s gaucho epic.</em></h1>
      <p class="en-lead">A horseman, an outlaw, a singer—and a voice that helped shape a nation's literary imagination. Discover the poem, the history behind it, and the people who inhabited its world.</p>
      <div class="en-actions"><a class="en-button" href="/leer/ida/1/" lang="es">Read the original poem <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="en-outline" href="#explore">Explore the story ↓</a></div>
      <p class="en-language-note">The full poem is currently available in its original Spanish. English-language historical guides and documentation are being developed independently.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-advice-feature"><div><p class="en-kicker">FAMILY · WISDOM · ORIGINAL VERSES</p><h2 id="en-advice-feature">Martín Fierro’s Advice to His Sons</h2><p>Read the complete Spanish verses of Canto XXXII with English commentary on brotherhood, honesty, friendship and work.</p><a href="/en/martin-fierro-advice-to-his-sons/">Read the advice and commentary ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-library-feature"><div><p class="en-kicker">THE POPULAR LIBRARY · ENGLISH</p><h2 id="en-library-feature">Literary Analysis, Argentine Literature and Gaucho Glossary</h2><p>Read critical guides, explore the history of gauchesque writing, and learn sixteen essential terms with their meanings in English.</p><a href="/en/library/">Explore the popular library ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-summary-seo"><div><p class="en-kicker">READER'S GUIDES · ORIGINAL ENGLISH RESEARCH</p><h2 id="en-summary-seo">Martín Fierro Summary and English Translation Guide</h2><p>Follow the plot of both parts, understand the major characters and find the historical English translation without confusing it with the original Spanish text.</p><a href="/en/martin-fierro-summary/">Read the plot summary ↗</a> · <a href="/en/martin-fierro-english-translation/">Explore the English translation ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-magazine-feature"><div><p class="en-kicker">CULTURAL MAGAZINE · IN ENGLISH</p><h2 id="en-magazine-feature">English studies of Argentine traditions and rural life</h2><p>Explore mate, gaucho clothing, payadas, rural traditions, the frontier, pulperías and the Day of Tradition.</p><a href="/en/magazine/">Read the English cultural magazine ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-archive-feature"><div><p class="en-kicker">ENGLISH DOCUMENTARY COLLECTION</p><h2 id="en-archive-feature">The Historical Archive: Manuscripts, Letters, and Early Editions</h2><p>Read documentary descriptions in English and consult original institutional sources.</p><a href="/en/archive/">Explore the archive ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-characters-feature"><div><p class="en-kicker">ENGLISH READING COMPANION</p><h2 id="en-characters-feature">Meet the Characters of Martín Fierro</h2><p>Explore eleven characters, their place in the story, and the cantos where they appear.</p><a href="/en/characters/">Explore the character guide ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-author-feature"><div><p class="en-kicker">NEW · BIOGRAPHY IN ENGLISH</p><h2 id="en-author-feature">José Hernández: The Writer Behind Martín Fierro</h2><p>Discover the life, journalism, political convictions, and rural experiences of the Argentine author through a documentary biography.</p><a href="/en/jose-hernandez/">Read the biography ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-gaucho-article"><div><p class="en-kicker">NEW · ENGLISH HISTORY & CULTURE</p><h2 id="en-gaucho-article">Who Were the Argentine Gauchos? History, Life, and Culture on the Pampas</h2><p>Discover the workers, horsemen, rural traditions, social conflicts, and literary legacy behind Argentina's iconic gaucho.</p><a href="/en/argentine-gauchos/">Read the historical guide ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="en-first-feature" aria-labelledby="en-first-article"><div><p class="en-kicker">NEW · ENGLISH READER’S GUIDE</p><h2 id="en-first-article">What Is Martín Fierro? Story, Meaning, and Historical Background</h2><p>Start with the story of the gaucho whose song became a defining work of Argentine literature. An English introduction to both parts, the historical context, and the questions the poem still raises.</p><a href="/en/what-is-martin-fierro/">Read the complete guide ↗</a></div></section>\n  <section class="en-introduction" aria-labelledby="en-intro">
    <p class="en-kicker">A LITERARY LANDMARK</p>
    <h2 id="en-intro">Why does <em>Martín Fierro</em> matter?</h2>
    <div><p>Published in two parts in 1872 and 1879, José Hernández’s narrative poem follows a gaucho whose encounters with military authority, rural life, and violence reveal the tensions of nineteenth-century Argentina.</p><p>Its first-person voice, rhythms, and rural vocabulary made it a defining work of gauchesque literature. Beyond the adventures of one man, the poem raises enduring questions about justice, belonging, and the treatment of people living at society’s margins.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section id="explore" class="en-section" aria-labelledby="en-explore">
    <div class="en-section-heading"><p class="en-kicker">EXPLORE THE COLLECTION</p><h2 id="en-explore">Begin with the people, places, and pages.</h2></div>
    <div class="en-grid">{items.map(item=><article class="en-card"><a href={item.href}><div class="en-image"><img src={item.image} alt={item.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></div><div class="en-card-copy"><span>{item.n} / THE COLLECTION</span><h3>{item.title}</h3><p>{item.summary}</p><strong>Explore this section <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></strong>{item.href.startsWith('/leer/') && <small>Original Spanish poem</small>}</div></a></article>)}</div>
    <p class="en-context">The original poem remains in Spanish; the historical guides, character profiles, and archive descriptions are available in English.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="en-translation" aria-labelledby="en-translations"><div><p class="en-kicker">THE POEM ACROSS LANGUAGES</p><h2 id="en-translations">An English translation with its own history.</h2><p>Walter Owen’s historical English translation appeared in 1935. A digitized edition can be consulted through Wikimedia Commons. Its text is not reproduced in our reader; translations have their own copyright considerations.</p><div class="en-actions"><a class="en-button" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Gaucho_Martin_Fierro_-_Jose_Hernandez.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the historical edition ↗</a><a class="en-outline" href="/traducciones/">Translation sources (Spanish) ↗</a></div></div><img src="/media/archivo/ejemplar-corregido-martin-fierro-1872-portada.webp" alt="Historical cover of the original 1872 Spanish edition of El gaucho Martín Fierro; not the Walter Owen translation" loading="lazy"/></section>
  <section class="en-final" aria-labelledby="en-final-title"><p class="en-kicker">A DOCUMENTARY CULTURAL LIBRARY</p><h2 id="en-final-title">A poem is only the beginning.</h2><p>Martín Fierro lives at the meeting point of literature, social history, and the cultural memory of Argentina. This independent edition brings sources and stories together while clearly distinguishing historical evidence from modern interpretation.</p><a href="/fuentes-y-derechos/">Sources, attribution, and editorial transparency (Spanish) ↗</a></section>
</div>
